# Language Consistency

Use English as the default language for repository artifacts unless the user or project explicitly requests another language.

## Default Rule

- Write code identifiers, filenames, folders, comments, tests, commit messages, documentation, rules, skills, templates, scripts, and examples in English.
- Do not mix English and another language in the same artifact without a deliberate reason.
- Keep domain vocabulary in English even when business stakeholders use another language, unless the project has approved canonical domain terms in that language.
- Prefer one consistent spelling and terminology set across APIs, DTOs, events, logs, docs, and tests.

## Allowed Exceptions

- Generate content in another language when the user explicitly asks for it, such as Brazilian Portuguese (`pt-BR`).
- Preserve legal names, product names, provider field names, user-facing copy, and third-party contract terms in their required language.
- Keep localized user-facing messages in locale-specific files or translation resources, not scattered through application logic.

## Review Checklist

- New files and changed text use English by default.
- User-requested non-English output is consistently written in the requested language.
- No artifact mixes languages accidentally.
- Localized text is isolated behind the project's i18n or content-management pattern.
